      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare the analgesic requirement in the post-operative period after closure or non-closure of the peritoneum at the caesarean delivery with a standardised anaesthetic and surgical technique."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A randomised double-blind controlled trial was performed on 100 women who underwent elective caesarean delivery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Analgesic requirement assessed by morphine usage via patient controlled analgesia pump over the first 24-hour period after surgery, oral analgesia used during the first four days, postoperative pain assessed by a visual analogue scale and a verbal rating scale, and patient satisfaction assessed by verbal rating scale were the main outcome measures."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Pain scores at 24 hours were similar in both groups (43.5 in closure and 40.5 in non-closure) but during the first 24 hours the non-closure group had used significantly less morphine than the closure group (0.64 mg/kg of body weight vs 0.82 mg/kg, P = 0.04). The patients in non-closure group had significantly higher satisfaction scores after 24 hours than the closure group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Non-closure of both visceral and parietal peritoneum at the caesarean section produces a significant reduction in the post-operative use of patient controlled analgesia pump morphine and significantly higher patient satisfaction at 24 hours post-operatively."
        }
      ]},
